lazysite (0.10.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * EDGE build on 0.10.2. No BREAKING change and no migration. Edge sites take it
    next update; beta/stable stay on their channel until it promotes.
  * SM238 per-domain tools over MCP: list_domains, domain_set and preview_domain,
    plus a host parameter on activate_theme/activate_layout that scopes the
    instance-wide call to a single domain. A partner previously needed an API
    token issued purely to reach domain-set.
  * SM239 MCP/control-API action parity is now enforced by a lint mapping every
    API action to its MCP tool, with API-only and MCP-only entries carrying
    reasons. It found 13 API actions missing from the capability model's unlocks
    lists, so describe_capabilities had been under-reporting every one of those
    capabilities.
  * SM240 upload_file writes binary content over MCP through the same locks,
    content history and ACL checks as every other write.
  * SM247 a missing parameter is no longer a destructive instruction:
    theme-activate with the name in the wrong parameter sanitised to the empty
    string, which meant deactivate, and returned ok:1 after stripping a live
    site's theme. Empty is now an error; deactivation needs deactivate=1.
  * SM243 warnings at the point of writing rather than only in the briefing:
    document bodies, style blocks and baked chrome in pages; chrome-hiding or
    default-transparent themes; and @group ACLs, which match manager users only.
    All warn, none refuse. A rename also reports the alias its retired URL needs.
  * SM244 audit_site reports the starter pages still present and how many are in
    the sitemap, reading a provenance marker nothing had ever read.
  * SM255 one write path for lazysite.conf. Seven write sites across five modules
    wrote the same file by their own mechanisms and only one recorded the write,
    so a config change appeared in content history while a domain registration, a
    plugin toggle, a theme activation, a layouts_repo change and a setup-manager
    run did not. Two were unsafe as well as unrecorded: the theme and layout
    pointer setters wrote the live layout:/theme: keys non-atomically and
    unlocked, which a lock-free reader can observe truncated. All seven now use
    the one writer, so installing a layout, activating a theme or layout, setting
    layouts_repo, toggling or configuring a plugin, and setup-manager all gain
    content-history entries. Commits raised from a plugin hook were authored
    "unknown" and are now attributed. A new lint asserts that nothing writes or
    commits that file outside the single writer.
  * Removes a literal NUL byte from a shipped appearance page, which had made git
    treat it as binary, and adds a lint barring control characters in pages.

